 <office:automatic-styles/>
 <office:body>
  <office:text>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Hardware Verification Using Model Checking and Formal Logic: An Experience Report</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">I. Introduction</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>A. In this presentation I will describe hardware verification using model checking and formal logic. I will compare and contrast these two methodologies and highlight their csimilarities and differences. I intend to show that both methodologies are fundamentally equivalent - i.e. that model checking is a special case of theorem proving. Specifically, model checking corresponds to a proof technique called case analysis. Additionally, I will identify the practical and theoretical limitations of these methods and explain how these methodologies can be combined to in a complementary fashion to mitigate these deficiencies. Finally, I will close by presenting a case study in which model checking and formal logic were used successfully to verify a portion of the Kami processor and other components used within SiFive’s S-series processors</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">II. Background</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>A. Verification and Validation</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>1. Definition</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="5"/>a. Hardware verification is the practice of ensuring that a hardware system conforms to a specification. A hardware system specification prescribes a set of constraints that must by satisfied by a conformant system. Validation is the practice of ensuring that the constraints prescribed by a system specification encapsulate the behavior and properties needed for the system to fulfill its intended purpose. IEEE defines system verification as follows. [CITATION].</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="5"/>b. As a result of their common goals, all hardware verification methodologies must perform certain common activities: they must define <text:s text:c="1"/>the intended behavior of the system. A processing system is a system that transforms some set of inputs to another set of outputs. This mapping corresponds to a mathematical function possessing an input range and an output domain. This behavioral function can be represented by a set of input/output pairs. This set, in turn, represents the functional behavior of the system in question. Additionally, a system specification may constrain the additional attributes of a computation process, such as its execution time and memory resources. As physical systems, hardware systems additionally may be constrained by area, and power consumption constraints. Collectively, those constraints that impinge on the system’s ability to perform its intended function comprise the system’s specification. The activity of ensuring that a system specification encompasses those constraints needed to ensure that a compliant system will be able to satisfy its intended function is called Validation.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Every verification methodology starts with specification - the formal definition of the constraints that must be satisfied by a conformant system. These specifications must define the functional behavior of the system - its input domain and output range and the mapping between them - and constrain additional computation properties such as time, memory, area, and power consumption. Once these constraints have been formulated, every verification methodology must formally model the system being verified. At Apple, hardware systems are modeled using System Verilog. At SiFive, we modeled systems Each formalization using mathematical systems of equations, using the high-level hardware modeling language, Kami, and occassionally using System Verilog. Each representation has an associated level of abstraction which captures different aspects of the system being modeled. This abstraction level, restricts the range of attributes that we can effectively represent in out model representation, but may also simplify analysis by abstracting out attributes that we need not concern ourselves with at a given point.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Once we have modeled the system, every hardware methodology requires us to translate the constraints prescribed by the system specification into model constraints. For example, System verilog assertions. Once this is done, each methodology requires us to apply a set of methods to verify that the model indeed conforms to the prescribed constraints. This process varies based on the methodology used. Model checking uses a variant of stimulus testing in which inputs are fed into the model and the output it observed to infer that the internal behavior of the system under analysis. All of hardware verification methods become increasingly difficult to apply as the unit under analysis grows in size/complexity. Accordingly, all of the methods in widespread use require us to decompose larger systems into subunits that can be verified independently. By decomposing larger systems into modules - subunits that interact with each other through well defined and stable interfaces - we are able to use the principles of abstraction, encapsulation, and decomposition to simplify our system analysis. However, once we decompose a system into submodules, we must then recombine subunit verifications to derive assurances for the system as a whole.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>B. Illustrative Examples</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>1. Throughout the rest of this presentation I will be using a simple hardware system to illustrate the use of model checking and of formal logic for hardware verification.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>In figure [FIGURE], I present a state transition diagram for a simple hardware module that accepts a binary number over a serial bus and outputs a 1 iff the number transmitted is divisible by 3. As the unit receives bits on its serial bus, it updates it internal state to one of three states. These states are labeled, 0, 1, and 2. The device maintains a simple invariant in which the current state is congruent to the number received so far mod 3. For example, the bit sequence [1, 0] represents the binary number 2, which is congruent to 2 mod 3. When <text:s text:c="1"/>reset, the device enters state 0. Upon receiving the first bit, it transitions to state 1, because the number received represents the number 1 which is congruent to 1 mod 3. Next the module receives a 0, so the bits represent the binary number 2, which is congruent to 2 mod 3. Accordingly the device moves to state 2. If the device is in state 0, it outputs a 1 indicating that the number received is divisible by 3.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>In figure [FIGURE], I present Kami code that implements this device. In this formalization, you see that the device contains a 2 bit register capable of storing three state values and has three ports: a serial input line, a single bit output port, and a reset line. When the reset line is held low, the device reads a bit from the input port on the leading edge of each clock signal. Additionally, it outputs a signal indicating whether or not the currently received value is divisible by 3.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>all of the source code for this example, including the model checking verification and formal proofs are available from GitHub (in a private repository) at: https://github.com/llee454/fsm.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>2. In addition, throughout the presentation I will be refering to an open source verification project that I completed successfully at SiFive. Links to this project can be found at https://larrylee.tech. In 2018, I was asked to verify the floating point unit of SiFive’s S-series cores. SiFive, had used model checking to test their floating point unit, but the implementation they were using was widely considered opaque. It had been written in a highly obfuscated style and the engineering team doubted its correctness. I abstracted the implementation onto a set of recurrence relations, then mapped those recurrence relations onto a canonical model for the division and square root operations. Then proved that the canonical model was correct. Afterwards, I translated the physical constraints associated with the device into value constraints for my model and then verified that the model satisfied these value constraints. This example illustrates the various levels of abstraction that may be employed while verifying hardware designs. And emphasizes the importance of selecting an appropriate level of abstraction when addressing specific questions.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>C. Model Checking</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>1. Model checking is the practice of verifying that a hardware design is correct by observing the design’s response to a broad range of inputs. Ideally, the inputs used in a model checking verification would be exhaustive - i.e. every possible combination of input and output values would be observed. However, model checking is constrained by combinatorial explosion. The set of input/output combinations for even modestly sized functional domains and ranges are too large to test exhaustively, and the size of the verification space grows exponentially with the dimensions of the functional domain and range.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>2. Specification: Every formal verification process starts with specification - formally defining the input/ouput domains and specifying the operating constraints that must be satisfied by the system. In model checking these specification can be either property based or reference model based. In property based formal verification, we identify a set of logical propositions that characterize the input and output domains. Ideally, the set of input and output propositions are specific enough to uniquely define the system’s function set. However, in many applications, the output value returned by the system in response for input values that fall outside of the system’s input domain are undefined. Alternately, the functional behavior of a system may be embodied by a reference implementation. At SiFive, we used the idea of a “transparently correct implementation” as part of our model checking verification activities. Given a reference implementation, we effectively, declare that the input/output behavior of the reference implementation effectively defined the behavior function of our implementation. In practice, a “transparently correct implementation” is an evaluble model of the system that defines the function space of the system in a naive or obvious way whereas our target implementation may contain optimizations that render static analysis difficult.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>At SiFive, we utilized both formalization methods when performing model checking. In the case study presented below, I describe how we used property based specification to define the correct behavior of the Floating point square root and division unit used at SiFive. Once we defined these properties we developed a Kami model that we then compiled into System Verilog. This then provided a canonical reference implementation that we then used to verify the a more obscure implementation written in the hardware language, Chisel.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>Constraint Translation: Once we have formalized the system specification, every hardware verification methodology must translate the specified functional constraints into model constraints. In the case property based specification in System Verilog, these constraints may be encoded as System Verilog Assertions. In Kami, the logical propositions can be expressed directly as logical assertions within Gallina, the dependently typed programming language in which Kami is embedded. When using canonical reference implementations, this process involves creating a model in the modeling language itself. While at SiFive, I developed several such models. Significantly, the Kami Processor was created to provide a canonical reference model for the RISC-V community. Significantly, the translation of specification constraints may express itself as the input/output values selected for testing.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>Derivation: Combinatorial explosion effectively precludes exhaustive verification for large designs. Consequently, model checking relies on <text:s text:c="1"/>a variety of strategies for strategically selecting testing inputs and for combining these strategically chosen input with generalized smoke testing to ensure adequate domain coverage. The primary principle of input selection is to identify edge cases, points within the input domain at or near behavioral boundaries - points within the input domain near behavioral discontinuities within the output codomain. Additionally, static analysis may be used to identify branching conditions and to correlate input values with execution paths within the system. Ideally, we aim to ensure that our test suites ensure complete path coverage through the system using boundary values to detect anomolous behavior at points within the program domain in which design errors are likely to concentrate.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>2. Broadly, two techniques are widely used to identify critical values and edge cases. Static code analysis and domain/codomain analysis. In static code analysis we examine the source code that describes our system and systematically, try to identify branch points, potential overflows (especially at comparisons), and execution paths. We then try to ensure that we test the system at values that trigger branching, that we select values to ensure execution of all execution paths, and that occur near critical domains.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>3. Composition: Each test verifies that a subunit within the system satisfies some constraint. In all but the simplest systems, these verifications have to be combined into an assertion for the system as a whole. This process of verification composition (alternately, proof composition), is an essential process for extrapolating from independent test results to an assurance for the system as a whole. In model checking, barring the rare instances in which exhaustive testing is possible, we rely on a divide and conquer strategy in which we decompose our model into modules. Each module is characterized by an internal behavior and an interface through which it interacts with the other modules that comprise our target system. We use stimulus testing to verify that the module operates correctly over the range of inputs and execution contexts associated with its input/output interface and these then reason over the composition of these subunits using the invariants preserved by their interfaces to conclude that the combined system conforms to its specification.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>B. The model checking methodology can be illustrated using the simple example introduced in the last section. Using the process outlined above, we begin by defining a set of properties that define the functional domain. The specification for the divisibility checker can be defined as follows: forall bs : list Bit, divisible? (decode bs) 3 &lt;-&gt; output (device bs) = 1. Using the property based definition of the input output domain is sufficient to uniquely define the functional domain uniquely. The next step is to model the system in question. Here we use the Kami representation presented in figure [FIGURE]. Once we have modeled the system, we must translate our constraints into model constrints. Here we map the input and output referenced in the specification onto specific port values.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>B. Illustrative Example Model Checking (add to the above)</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="1"/>C. Formal Logic</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"> <text:s text:c="3"/>1. Formally verified software development is the practice of writing mathematical proofs to verify that software is correct. Analogously, mathematical proofs can be used to verify that hardware designs are correct. A number of programming languages support reasoning about software. I specialize in using Coq to model systems, to formalize logical assertions about those systems, to derive mathematical proofs verifying logical propositions, and to verify that those mathematical proofs are logically sound. A mathematical proofs is a sequence of statements in which every statement is either a logical axiom, of follows from one or more of the preceding statements by applying a logical deduction rule. A formal language consists of a syntax and a semantics. A syntax consists of a set of tokens and a grammar for generating terms. A semantics is a function mapping terms onto values within a denotation set. A formal logic language is a formal language in which we have the fundamental logic values, true and false, a minimum set of logical connectors, such as conjunction, disjunction, implication, etc, and a semantics function that enables us to map expressions onto truth values. There is an isomorphism between mathematical proofs expressed in intuitionist logic and the terms of typed lambda calculus. This isomorphism is referred to as the Curry-Howard isomorphism. This relation equates implication with lambda abstraction, theorem application with function application, propositions with types, and proofs with the values that inhabit a given type. Coq, exploits this relation between terms within typed lambda calculus and proofs within intuitionist logic to define an language in which we can express algorithms, logical propositions, and mathematical proofs. This language is called Gallina. Coq additionally defines a macro language named Ltac, that can be used to generate Gallina terms, and a command language called Vernacular, that can be used to add definitions, add declarations, change configuration values, etc.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">As discussed above, every verification methodology must perform the following operations: specification, modeling, constraint translation, derivation, and composition.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">When using formal logic for specification, we encode the system constraints and requirements into a set of logical propositions. A specification may take the form of a set of logical assertions and propositions, or we may define a canonical model of the system that we are seeking to verify and then prove that the model of our implementation is equivalent to our canonical model.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Once we have defined a specification for the system, we must then model the system. Using formal logic, we may choose a variety of representations. While verifying the floating point unit of the Sifive’s S-series cores, I modeled the unit as a set of recurrence relations. While verifying components used within Kami Processor, I represented them as Kami expressions. I have modeled our divisibility checker as both a Kami module and as an abstract finite state machine. <text:s text:c="2"/>In the code sample given, I have verified the abstract finite state machine, and subsequently, proved that the traces produced by the Kami module are isomorphic to the traces generated by the finite state machine. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">After we model the system, we must then translate the specification into model constraints. When I verified the floating point unit, I had to ensure that the value stored within one of the unit’s registers were always small enough to fit within the space allocated for the register. When I translated this constraint, I had to apply the isomorphic mapping between the values stored in the implementation’s registers to compute the equivalent value expressed in my canonical implementation model. Similarly, in the divisibility checker module, I translate the abstract constraint that the abstract output of the system corresponds to the Kami value emitted by a specific Kami method call.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Once we have translated our specification constraints into specific model constraints we must then derive proofs that these constraints are satisfied by the model. Mathematical theorem proving involves the construction of mathematical proofs. A mathematical proof can be visualized as a tree structure whose leaves correspond to mathematical axioms and whose nodes correspond to derived assertions. Each node corresponds to the application of a single logical deduction rule. Every deduction rule is a formal rewrite rule that transforms one or more statements into a new derived statement. The children of each node correspond to those statements that the deduction rule associated with the node were applied to. There are a small number of logical deduction rules. Generally every logical connective has a set of introduction and elimination rules. Loosely speaking, theorem proving tactics correspond to the application of one or more of these deduction rules. For example, “proof by contradiction” corresponds to the application of False_ind in Gallina. Whereas “case analysis” corresponds to the use of a match statement. Accordingly, the process of derivation when verifying hardware designs using formal logic literally involves the construction of proof terms through the application of theorem proving tactics. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">This process is illustrated in figure [FIGURE] in which I present an excerpt of my proof that the abstract finite state machine model of the divisibility checker is correct. In this proof, I essentially use “case analysis” to verify that each of the transitions associated with the machine preserves a critical invariant. In each case I apply a set of equations concerning modular arithmetic and then combine the subproofs into a proof that establishes that the module is correct by exploiting a theorem concerning finite state machines generally.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">A similar example can be seen by examining the sample proof that the floating point unit is correct. Here I use a variety of theorem proving tactics such as mathematical induction, proof by contradiction, and proof by cases while exploiting theorems from real analysis. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">At this point it is appropriate to highlight that exhaustive model checking is logically equivalent to the tactic of proof by case analysis. In case analysis, we verify that a property holds for a system by proving that the the property holds for every possible value that can be assumed. Whenever a system’s functional domain is small enough to effectively enumerate, we can use case analysis effectively. For instance, when I verified that the divisibility checker is correct, I used case analysis to verify the machine for every possible input/state combination.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">As observed earlier however, exhaustive model checking is not feasible for large functional domains, and is logically impossible for infinite domains. In these instances, proof techniques such as mathematical induction enable us to establish results that are strictly impossible to establish using model checking.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Having derived proofs that verify that subunits satisfy some of the constraints imposed by a specification, we must then integrate these results into a composite proof concerning the system as a whole. Here the structure of mathematical proofs inherently supports compositions. Since mathematical proofs posses inherent tree structures and logical deduction rules are inherently compositional, composing subproofs to derive system-level proofs is fully natural using formal logic. Indeed every proof given in the examples so far involved composing smaller lemmas and assertions to generate broader composite claims.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">Here, it’s worth observing that model checking implicitly relies on formal logic to compose verifications of submodules into proofs that a system composed of these submodules posses the desired properties. Having talked with Prakash, it appears that this process of compositional reasoning is currently informal at Apple, but this may be a promising domain for integrating tools such as Coq, APL, Lean, etc. Model checking can be used to establish properties for submodules, and then these properties can be asserted as axioms in a theorem prover which can be used to formally prove composite results.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal">III. In Depth Case Studies</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Normal"/>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
</office:document>
